WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.145 --> 00:00:02.520
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:06.080
I help some sports fans fix
their caching performance.

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:07.932
Will we hit a performance
buzzer beater?

00:00:07.932 --> 00:00:08.890
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:11.680
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:13.780
Geeky Sports is
a website devoted

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:16.480
to the latest and greatest
regarding your favorite sci-fi

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:18.160
fantasy-based sports.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:21.370
Yes, they even have coverage
of that wizard-based broomstick

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:23.680
soccer game with the
flying locust thing

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:24.730
that people try to catch.

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:27.160
Anyhow, their engineering
team reached out

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:30.040
after they deployed a new
CDN and thumbnailing service,

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:32.860
only to see a massive
"cache fill" cost show up

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:33.730
on their cloud bill.

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:36.650
Yet no performance improvement's
showing up for their customers.

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:38.590
Now, this all sounds
like the right setup.

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:40.580
So let's try to figure
out what's going on.

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:42.700
Firstly, to check that
the assets were actually

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:45.130
being cached, we took a look
at the logging information

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:47.410
for the load balancer
in the cloud console.

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:48.910
Each request that's
logged will have

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:51.520
information detailing if
it properly had a cache hit

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:52.280
or not.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:54.670
For example, we can see that
the first request resulted

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:57.100
in a lookup, a
failure, and a fill.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.620
Meanwhile, our second
request to the same resource

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:01.780
results in just a cache hit.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:03.580
Looking back at the
logs for Geeky Sports,

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:05.080
we can see that
the number of cache

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:08.350
hit entries is really low for
the traffic during the day.

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:11.080
So it seems that the requests
were getting cached but not

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:14.870
being re-used by future
requests, hence the high cache

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:16.242
fill bill.

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:17.950
Now, let's think about
this for a second.

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:20.800
At their core, CDNs are really
just massively distributed

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:21.730
hash tables.

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:23.890
When a request
comes into the CDN,

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:27.300
it converts the URI of that
request to a cache key.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:30.010
If that key matches anything
that's already been cached,

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:32.770
then it just returns
the associated object.

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:35.020
Now, by default,
Google Cloud CDN

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:38.800
uses the complete request
URI as the cache key,

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:40.960
which means if you change
the protocol, like moving

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:44.770
from HTTP to HTTPS, you're
not going to get a cache hit.

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:46.930
And likewise, if the
query string is different,

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:48.430
you won't get a match either.

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:51.160
And that last one seems
to be the important one.

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:52.720
Looking back at
the logged request,

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:56.650
Geeky Sports is passing the
user token as a URL param, which

00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:58.900
means that each user
was causing each asset

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:02.740
to be cached by the CDN
individually, rather than

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:04.810
being shared between users.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:06.490
Thankfully for
our geeky friends,

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:08.169
the fix is pretty
straightforward.

00:02:08.169 --> 00:02:11.080
Using Google Cloud
CDN's custom cache keys,

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:12.820
we can modify how
the CDN matches

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:14.920
URIs for specific
assets and tell

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:16.810
it to ignore the query string.

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:19.450
Looking at the logs, we can
see that even though the user

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:22.870
ID is different, we got a
proper hit on the asset.

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:25.570
To learn more about how Geeky
Sports decreased their costs,

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:27.880
check out the related article
linked in the description

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:28.520
below.

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:30.644
And don't forget to check
out the rest of our Cloud

00:02:30.644 --> 00:02:32.290
Performance Atlas
content, because when

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:34.570
it comes to performance,
every millisecond counts.

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:39.285
